+They held eight independent exhibitions between 1874 and 1886. The group was never stable — constant fights about who to include, whether to also submit to the Salon, money, egos. But the principle held: we show our own work, on our own terms.
+
+And the response did begin to change. The mockery never disappeared, but supportive critics were writing seriously about the new painting by the late 1870s. Collectors started buying work from across the group. Dealers gave the artists solo exhibitions, and the large Impressionist show Paul Durand-Ruel brought to New York in 1886 reached a new public. Acceptance did not arrive all at once, and it did not arrive equally: some members found money and recognition while others continued to struggle. But by the time the final group exhibition closed, "Impressionist" no longer meant only a joke.
 
 
 
